.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt NQENC 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqenc ,
.Nm nqencd ,
.Nm nqdec ,
.Nm nqkkg ,
.Nm nqkkgd ,
.Nm nqksize ,
.Nm nqkname ,
.Nm nqkfind
.Nd ML-KEM and HQC key encapsulation
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqkkg
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqkkgd
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *seed" "size_t seedlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqenc
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqencd
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *pk" "size_t pklen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *seed" "size_t seedlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqdec
.Fa "nqkalg alg"
.Fa "const uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *ct" "size_t ctlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *ss" "size_t sslen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn nqksize "nqkalg alg" "nqksz *sz"
.Ft const char *
.Fn nqkname "nqkalg alg"
.Ft int
.Fn nqkfind "const char *name" "nqkalg *alg"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A key encapsulation mechanism lets anyone with a public key make a
ciphertext and a 32-byte shared secret, which only the holder of the
secret key can recover from the ciphertext.
noqtis provides ML-KEM (FIPS 203) and HQC (version 5.0.0 of 2025-08-22).
.Pp
.Vt nqkalg
names the parameter set:
.Bl -column "NQMLKEM1024" "ML-KEM-1024" "pk" "sk" "ciphertext" "ss" -offset indent
.It Sy constant Ta Sy name Ta Sy pk Ta Sy sk Ta Sy ciphertext Ta Sy ss
.It Dv NQMLKEM512 Ta ML-KEM-512 Ta 800 Ta 1632 Ta 768 Ta 32
.It Dv NQMLKEM768 Ta ML-KEM-768 Ta 1184 Ta 2400 Ta 1088 Ta 32
.It Dv NQMLKEM1024 Ta ML-KEM-1024 Ta 1568 Ta 3168 Ta 1568 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC128 Ta HQC-128 Ta 2241 Ta 2321 Ta 4433 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC192 Ta HQC-192 Ta 4514 Ta 4602 Ta 8978 Ta 32
.It Dv NQHQC256 Ta HQC-256 Ta 7237 Ta 7333 Ta 14421 Ta 32
.El
.Pp
.Fn nqksize
stores the sizes in
.Fa *sz ,
an
.Vt nqksz
with the members
.Va pk ,
.Va sk ,
.Va ct
and
.Va ss .
.Fn nqkname
returns the name, or
.Dv NULL
for an unknown value;
.Fn nqkfind
finds the algorithm whose name is exactly
.Fa name .
.Pp
.Fn nqkkg
makes a key pair from the operating system's random source.
.Fn nqkkgd
makes it from the standard's seed: 64 bytes (d || z) for ML-KEM, 32 bytes
for HQC.
.Pp
.Fn nqenc
encapsulates to the public key
.Fa pk :
it writes the ciphertext to
.Fa ct
and the shared secret to
.Fa ss .
.Fn nqencd
takes the encapsulation randomness from
.Fa seed
instead, for test vectors: the 32-byte m for ML-KEM, and m || salt for
HQC (32, 40 or 48 bytes for HQC-128, -192, -256).
For ML-KEM the public key is checked as FIPS 203 section 7.2 requires.
.Pp
.Fn nqdec
recovers the shared secret from
.Fa ct
with the secret key
.Fa sk ;
for ML-KEM the key is first checked as section 7.3 requires.
A ciphertext of the right length that was not made for this key, or was
changed, gives a pseudorandom secret unrelated to the sender's and
returns
.Dv NQOK
(implicit rejection, as both standards require); only malformed sizes
fail.
Protocols must therefore confirm the secret, for example by using it in an
authenticated cipher.
.Pp
Output buffers may be larger than the algorithm's sizes; only the defined
number of bytes is written.
Keys and ciphertexts given as input must have exactly the algorithm's
sizes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Every function except
.Fn nqkname
returns
.Dv NQOK
(0) or a negative error code:
.Bl -tag -width NQENOSUP
.It Bq Er NQEARG
.Fa alg
is not an
.Vt nqkalg ,
a pointer is
.Dv NULL ,
or a
.Fa seed
does not have the standard's length.
.It Bq Er NQEBUF
An output buffer is smaller than the algorithm's size.
.It Bq Er NQEDEC
An input key or ciphertext does not have the algorithm's length, or a
key fails the standard's input check.
.It Bq Er NQERNG
The operating system's random source failed
.Po
.Fn nqkkg ,
.Fn nqenc ,
and
.Fn nqdec
for ML-KEM, whose masked decapsulation draws fresh mask shares
.Pc .
.It Bq Er NQENOSUP
.Fn nqkfind :
no algorithm has that name.
.El
.Pp
On any failure, the output buffers are zeroed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The program
.Pa docs/examples/kem.c
of the source tree, with the sizes taken at run time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nqksz z;
uint8_t *pk, *sk, *ct, a[32], b[32];
int rc = nqksize(NQMLKEM768, &z), same = 0;
if (rc != NQOK || z.ss != sizeof a)
  return 2;
pk = malloc(z.pk);
sk = malloc(z.sk);
ct = malloc(z.ct);
if (pk == NULL || sk == NULL || ct == NULL)
  return 2;
rc = nqkkg(NQMLKEM768, pk, z.pk, sk, z.sk);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqenc(NQMLKEM768, pk, z.pk, ct, z.ct, a, sizeof a);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqdec(NQMLKEM768, sk, z.sk, ct, z.ct, b, sizeof b);
if (rc == NQOK)
  same = nqcteq(a, b, sizeof a);
nqwipe(sk, z.sk);
nqwipe(a, sizeof a);
nqwipe(b, sizeof b);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-dec 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-enc 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqcteq 3 ,
.Xr nqwipe 3
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T FIPS 203: Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard
.%Q NIST
.%D August 2024
.Re
.Rs
.%T Hamming Quasi-Cyclic (HQC), version 5.0.0
.%D August 2025
.Re
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Key generation, encapsulation and decapsulation run in constant time with
respect to the seeds, the randomness, the secret key and the shared
secret; ML-KEM decapsulation is also first-order masked.
In HQC, a secret-derived bit becomes public in the three places its
specification allows, and nowhere else.
